Scott asks to finish the discussion of \(P\mathbf{N}\) from Example 7.8 by showing that the Exercise 5.14 combinators \(\mathrm{fun} : P\mathbf{N}\to(P\mathbf{N}\to P\mathbf{N})\) and \(\mathrm{graph} : (P\mathbf{N}\to P\mathbf{N})\to P\mathbf{N}\) are computable, and likewise \(\lambda x,y.\, x\cap y\), \(\lambda x,y.\, x\cup y\), and \(\lambda x,y.\, x+y=\{n+m\mid n\in x\text{ and }m\in y\}\), and to say which elements of \(P\mathbf{N}\) are computable. Neighbourhoods are \(\mathrm{nbhd}\, n\), the complement of the finite set of set bits of \(n\), which is Example 7.8's \(\mathbf{N}\setminus E_n\); the closure takes the presentation \(P\mathbf{N}\mathrm{pres}\) and its decidable inclusion test as given. Positive information is `elemSet`: \(k\) lies in it when some neighbourhood belonging to the element has bit \(k\) set, the union of the finite sets those neighbourhoods exclude. The equivalence in `Basic` answers the last question in both directions: an element of \(P\mathbf{N}\mathrm{pres}\) is computable if and only if membership in `elemSet` is recursively enumerable, one direction by conjoining the primitive-recursive bit test `bitAt` with the enumerable neighbourhood predicate, the other by quantifying that predicate over the primitive-recursive diagonal list `bitsCode n n`. Intersection is `capMap`, relating a product of neighbourhoods \(X,Y\) to \(Z\) when \(X\subseteq Z\) and \(Y\subseteq Z\), so the excluded set of \(Z\) sits inside both excluded sets; `capMap_rel_iff` is those two index inclusions and `capMap_isComputable` decides them by the presentation's inclusion test. Union is `cupMap`, relating the product to \(Z\) when \(X\cap Y\subseteq Z\); `compl_inter_nbhd` identifies the excluded set of that intersection with the union of the excluded sets, `cupMap_rel_iff` rewrites the relation as inclusion of the neighbourhood of the bitwise disjunction \(\mathrm{myLor}\, n\, m\), and `cupMap_isComputable` decides that inclusion. Minkowski sum is `plusMap`: the excluded set of \(Z\) is contained in `sumSet` of the two excluded sets, and `mem_sumSet` is the defining equation \(k=a+b\). `plusIdx` is the primitive-recursive bit code of that sum, `plusIdx_testBit` says its \(k\)-th bit is set exactly for such a pair of set bits, `plusMap_rel_iff` rewrites the relation as inclusion of \(\mathrm{nbhd}(\mathrm{plusIdx}\, n\, m)\), and `plusMap_isComputable` decides it. The sequence code of Exercise 5.14 is `tag`: `tag_eq_foldr` is the right fold sending an entry \(a\) and an accumulator to \(\mathrm{num}(a+1,\mathrm{accumulator})\), started at \(\mathrm{num}(0,m)\), so the tag of \([n_0,\ldots,n_{k-1}]\) at \(m\) is \([n_0+1,\ldots,n_{k-1}+1,0,m]\). `gMap` is the uncurried operation, requiring the excluded set of the output to lie in `Fun` of the two excluded sets, and `mem_Fun_compl_nbhd_iff` says \(j\) lies in that set exactly when some tag on the excluded set of the first neighbourhood has value \(j\) and list drawn from the excluded set of the second, which is \(\mathrm{fun}(u)(x)\). `funMap` is the currying of `gMap`, and `funMap_isComputable` applies the currying theorem to `gMap_isComputable`, whose predicate is the bounded test that every set bit of the output lies in `Fun`. `graphMap` relates a function-space neighbourhood \(W\) to \(Z\) when the excluded set of \(Z\) lies in `GraphIdx` \(W\); `mem_GraphIdx_iff` puts a code there when it is `tagOfBits` of a finite input for which every map in \(W\) sends the corresponding neighbourhood to one common output neighbourhood with that value bit set, the positive information shared by \(\mathrm{graph}(f)\) for all \(f\) in \(W\). `graphIdx_isComputable` enumerates that membership from the function-space inclusion decider, and `graphMap_isComputable` closes it under the bounded check of the excluded output codes. The numbered statement `example_723` records only computability of `graphMap`. The retraction identities \(\mathrm{fun}\circ\mathrm{graph}=\mathrm{id}\) and \(\mathrm{graph}\circ\mathrm{fun}\supseteq\mathrm{id}\) from Exercise 5.14 are not reproved; Exercise 7.23 asks for computability. **Theorem:** yes — \(\mathrm{fun}\), \(\mathrm{graph}\), intersection, union, and Minkowski sum are each proved computable, and the computable elements are those whose positive set is recursively enumerable; `example_723` itself states only the graph clause. **Proof:** yes — each relation reduces to a decidable inclusion or a bounded quantifier over the primitive-recursive bit, tag, and list operations, and both directions of the enumerability equivalence are proved.
